{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789498078,"nonce":"DlY6u+IYkxObt7ed7q6nLxqz1a8=","title":"Assigning a list slice in fannkuch","message":"After bound methods landed in 6fcc27b6ecbe62a4091ab8a6a3e4bf4bb51449d6, the unedited fannkuch kernel advances to `perm[:k+1] = perm[k::-1]` and zypy reports `slice is not supported yet` at the assignment target. The read `perm[k::-1]` already works and returns the expected reverse prefix. A minimal reproducer:\n\n    xs = [0, 1, 2, 3]\n    xs[:3] = xs[2::-1]\n    print(xs)      # CPython: [2, 1, 0, 3]\n\n`bind` treats every subscript target as an integer index and attempts to lower the `Slice` node as an expression. The implementation needs in-place replacement through the shared list header, so aliases see the change. For step 1, the replacement may be shorter or longer; for non-unit steps Python requires the replacement length to match the selected positions. The right side must be evaluated and saved before changing the destination, including self-assignment. A zero step raises ValueError. A kernel made to print a timing still needs its fannkuch answer checked against CPython.\n\nRelated: speed-center index 89aaf85 and harness 39c723b.","files":null}]}